espie@lain.home (Marc Espie) wrote:

> Before even trying vmware, have a look at VirtualPC.
> 
> Microsoft bought it a while ago, so that they could help people
> migrate to a new windows and keep their old machine running.
> 
> It is a free emulator, in the monetary sense (not free in the `free
> software' sense, though) that you can download as you wish and play
> with. 
> 
... and the reason it became free was because it has to compete against the 
free vmware player.

The features of the two systems seem pretty similar, I think the main 
difference is that with vmware player if you want to change settings you 
have to edit the settings in a text file whereas virtualPC and the other 
vmware tools give you a settings dialog.

I don't know how good virtualPC is at running Linux. I just created a new 
virtualPC virtual machine and told it to boot from the Ubuntu 6.1 cd. It 
seems to have switched to a graphics mode that virtualPC doesn't 
understand. ...

... Ok, I found this: 
http://weblogs.asp.net/pscott/archive/2005/10/13/427426.aspx
which suggests that the problem is that Ubuntu defaults to a graphics depth 
which virtualPC doesn't support and you can use the boot recovery console 
to change it.

So one difference is that vmware supports 24bit color depth, virtualPC only 
16bit. I guess that means you aren't going to get quite the same immersive 
quality graphics when playing nethack under virtualPC. :)


